{"name":"Heartwood","hex":"#6f4232","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"heartwood","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The wood nearer the heart of a stem or branch, different in color from the sapwood."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/heartwood"}],"entries":[{"title":"Heartwood (novel)","blocks":[{"text":"Heartwood is a thriller novel by American author Amity Gaige. It was published by Simon & Schuster on April 1, 2025. The novel centers the disappearance of a woman named Valerie Gillis. The novel alternates between the perspectives of three characters, with Valerie's chapters being written in the form of letters to her mother. The novel also features transcripts of tip-line messages and interviews with the warden, Lieutenant Beverly \"Bev\" Miller."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1c/Heartwood_novel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heartwood_(novel)","wikidata":"Q139261373"},{"title":"Heartwood (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Heartwood is a 1998 American independent drama film written and directed by Lanny Cotler and starring Jason Robards and Hilary Swank."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/5f/Heartwoodfilm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heartwood_(film)","wikidata":"Q1192438"},{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a structural tissue/material found as xylem in the stems and roots of trees and other woody plants. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumb